Return no data for tar entries that have no body, and read the GNU realsize field and the old
GNU sparse map only for sparse ('S') entries.

The tar reader zeroes entry_bytes_remaining for devices, FIFOs, symlinks and directories
(including "regular" entries whose name ends in '/'), and for old-style/GNU hard links whose
size field it ignores, but keeps disk_size = the header's size field. At the end of the
(empty) body archive_read_format_tar_read_data() reports EOF at offset disk_size, and
archive_read_data() pads zeros up to it: a 1.5 KiB FIFO entry with a base-256 size of 2^40
hands the caller 1 TiB of zeros (entry_limit allows up to 2^60). header_gnutar() also takes
realsize and the sparse map from every GNU header, so a regular entry with one data byte and a
large realsize or sparse offset does the same. The oss-fuzz harnesses read every body with
archive_read_data(), so these inputs time out (186 of the 189 libarchive hang inputs of the
2026-11 campaigns; the other 3 are pax GNU.sparse 1.0 files that declare a huge hole, which is
the format working as designed). Includes test_read_format_tar_nodata_size.
Not fixed upstream as of 432ab464 (no related issue or PR on 2026-11-20).

